steps.body:
Some table rules read the two entries before the current one. Say each item in a row is worth some points, and you may take any items except two neighbors. At each item you choose: skip it and keep the best so far, or take it and add it to the best from two items back, since its neighbor must be skipped. Let `dp[i]` be the best total from the first `i` items:

```javascript
const vals = [3, 8, 4, 2, 9];
const dp = new Array(vals.length + 1).fill(0); // dp[0]: no items, 0
dp[1] = vals[0];
for (let i = 2; i <= vals.length; i++) {
  dp[i] = Math.max(dp[i - 1], dp[i - 2] + vals[i - 1]); // skip it, or take it
}
console.log(dp.join(" ")); // 0 3 8 8 10 17
```

The table is one longer than the array, so `dp[i]` is about item `vals[i - 1]`, and `dp[0] = 0` gives the empty row its own entry. That shift is where off-by-one bugs hide, so write down what an entry means before you write the loop.

Each entry reads only the two before it, so two variables can replace the table. `prev` holds the entry two back and `curr` the one just before:

```javascript
const vals = [3, 8, 4, 2, 9];
let prev = 0; // dp[i - 2]
let curr = 0; // dp[i - 1]
for (const v of vals) {
  [prev, curr] = [curr, Math.max(curr, prev + v)];
}
console.log(curr); // 17
```

The destructuring assignment builds the right side from the old values before it assigns either one, so you need no temporary variable. Both versions take O(n) time. The table takes O(n) memory, and the two variables O(1).

