steps.body:
Now every item has a weight and a value, the bag holds at most `cap`, and each item goes in once or not at all. Let `dp[c]` be the best value that fits in capacity `c`. For each item, every capacity chooses: skip the item and keep `dp[c]`, or take it and add its value to the best for the room that's left, `dp[c - w]`:

```javascript
const items = [[3, 4], [4, 5], [2, 3]]; // [weight, value]
const cap = 6;
const dp = new Array(cap + 1).fill(0); // dp[c]: the best value within capacity c
for (const [w, v] of items) {
  for (let c = cap; c >= w; c--) {
    dp[c] = Math.max(dp[c], dp[c - w] + v); // skip it, or take it
  }
}
console.log(dp.join(" ")); // 0 0 3 4 5 7 8
```

Loop the capacity **down**, from `cap` to `w`. Then `dp[c - w]`, a smaller capacity, hasn't been updated for this item yet, so it still means "the best without this item". Going up, `dp[c - w]` may already include the item, and it gets packed again and again:

```javascript
const dp = new Array(7).fill(0);
const [w, v] = [2, 3]; // one item
for (let c = w; c <= 6; c++) { // wrong: upward
  dp[c] = Math.max(dp[c], dp[c - w] + v);
}
console.log(dp.join(" ")); // 0 0 3 3 6 6 9
```

One 2 kg item worth 3 shows up as 9 at capacity 6: three copies. The table costs O(n * cap) time for n items and O(cap) memory. Taking the best value per kilogram first doesn't work either, since a small valuable item can leave room that nothing else fills.

example.code:
const input = require("fs").readFileSync(0, "utf8");
const lines = input.trim().split("\n");
const [n, cap] = lines[0].trim().split(/\s+/).map(Number);

// dp[c]: the best value that fits in c kilograms
const dp = new Array(cap + 1).fill(0);
for (const line of lines.slice(1, n + 1)) {
  const [name, kgText, valueText] = line.trim().split(/\s+/);
  const kg = Number(kgText);
  const value = Number(valueText);
  for (let c = cap; c >= kg; c--) {
    dp[c] = Math.max(dp[c], dp[c - kg] + value);
  }
  console.log(`after ${name}: ${dp.join(" ")}`);
}
console.log(`best: ${dp[cap]}`);

